## Development

A Python {{cookiecutter.python_min_version}}+ uv workspace. `services/` and `libs/`
start empty. Each member owns its `src/<package>/`, tests, and `pyproject.toml`;
service members also include a Dockerfile and an application entry point to implement.
The root `tests/` directory is for integration tests.

```bash
make bootstrap
uv run pre-commit install
make new-service NAME=api
make new-lib NAME=common
uv add --package api ./libs/common
make bootstrap
make check
```

Names above are examples. Use lowercase Python identifiers; names must be unique
across the workspace. Creation commands refuse to overwrite existing members.
Add `DESCRIPTION="..."` to customize a member's description. From another
directory, use `make -C /path/to/workspace new-service NAME=api`.
Customize the independent scaffolds in
`templates/service/` and `templates/lib/`. Libraries have no Dockerfile or
application entry point. Both commands regenerate this workspace README from
`templates/readme/README.md.jinja`, which includes `header.md.jinja` for the tables
and badges. Edit those templates, then run `make codegen`; `make check-generated`
checks that the output matches. Run codegen after manually adding, removing, or
renaming members. Member READMEs contain direct badge links created by their
Cookiecutter templates and remain yours to edit.

The root `pyproject.toml` remains editable. Its pytest paths are `tests`,
`services/*/tests`, and `libs/*/tests`, so new members are discovered without
regenerating configuration. Add root tooling dependencies directly to this file.

Use `uv add --package <name> <dependency>` to add a member's dependencies. For
workspace dependencies, supply the local path as above; uv records the workspace
source automatically. Use `uv run --package <name> ...` to run a single member.
Commit the generated `uv.lock`. The root remains a virtual project.

`make bootstrap` installs all members and dev/docs tools, and creates `.env` from
`.env.example` if missing. Update `.env.example` when introducing settings.
`make lint`, `make format`, `make test`, `make docs`, and `make serve` are also
available. Add at least one member or integration test before `make check`;
pytest fails if the workspace has no tests.

Choose your web framework, task runner, and persistence dependencies per service.
The service entry point raises `NotImplementedError` until implemented.
See [infra/README.md](infra/README.md) for the empty Compose configuration and
container build instructions. `infra/k8s/` and `infra/nginx/` are placeholders.

GitHub uses `pull-request.yml` for PR checks and `ci.yml` for the repository's
default branch. Both run the lint, test, and documentation checks and retain
JUnit and coverage reports as `check-reports`. Default-branch CI uploads a
`github-pages` artifact and automatically deploys the documentation and reports.
Select **Settings → Pages → Build and deployment → Source: GitHub Actions** once
in your repository. Set `github_repository` to your `owner/repo` when generating
the workspace. `tool.codegen.reports_url` in `pyproject.toml` defaults to
its public Pages reports URL; change it and run `make codegen` for a custom domain.
Update existing member README links directly when changing the reports host.

`make lint` runs Ruff and its formatting check directly. `make test` runs pytest
with standard JUnit and coverage reports. Failed checks still publish test reports
when the docs build succeeds. PRs retain reports without deploying them.

The direct Shields badge links expect JSON endpoints at
`reports/badges/{services,libs}/<name>/{lint,tests,coverage}.json`. These endpoints
require a separate reporting integration; the Make targets do not generate them.
Endpoints must be publicly accessible to [Shields](https://shields.io/badges/endpoint-badge).
With an empty `reports_url`, badges display `not run`.

Optional Sourcecraft CI runs `make bootstrap` and `make check` and retains JUnit
and coverage reports. Pre-commit, MkDocs, and LICENSE are included.
